Superficial retained foreign body (marine based) from beach swimming to foot
Minor Trauma and Personal Injury
Sharp marine puncture pain, Visible dark entry point, Barefoot ocean wading mechanism

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: MTI-036
Reason for Visit: Acute foot pain following a marine puncture injury, requiring urgent evaluation for retained foreign body and envenomation.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage noted immediate, sharp marine puncture pain following barefoot ocean wading. Urgent clinical evaluation was necessary to assess for systemic envenomation, extract the retained organic material, and initiate prophylactic treatment to prevent aggressive marine bacterial infection prior to the availability of daytime clinical services.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: I stepped on something sharp in the ocean and there is a black spike stuck in my foot causing intense burning.
History of Present Illness: Patient provides a clear history of wading barefoot in a marine environment known to harbor sea urchins and stingrays. Patient experienced immediate sharp stinging and burning pain upon stepping into the sand. There is a subjective foreign body sensation described as distinct grit or needle-like pain upon palpation. Pain is severely exacerbated by direct pressure or attempted weight-bearing on the affected area. The patient denies any rapidly ascending systemic symptoms, muscle spasms, or respiratory distress.
Functional Status: Ambulating with a severe antalgic limp, avoiding forefoot loading.
Social/Safety Concerns: Minor impediment to mobility; no immediate environmental risks in the home.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 126/78 | HR: 84 | Temp: 36.8 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 75 Kg
General: Alert, in localized distress when the foot is manipulated or dependent.
Physical Exam: Plantar examination reveals a pinpoint entry wound presenting with a visible dark dot indicating the retained marine spine. There is localized intense erythema and minor edema strictly surrounding the puncture site. There is a lack of significant deep compartment swelling or rapidly ascending lymphangitis. Distal neurovascular examination of the affected extremity is intact. Minimal bleeding is noted, easily controlled with simple external pressure.
Environmental Observations: Patient’s wet beach gear localized to the bathroom; advised to clear walk spaces.
Medication Reconciliation: No routine medications. Immersed foot in hot water prior to arrival with partial pain relief.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Superficial retained foreign body (marine based) to foot - Status: Stable
Differentials:

Stingray envenomation (major) - Ruled out due to: Absence of a large, jagged laceration, profound tissue necrosis, and systemic autonomic instability.

Deep space plantar infection - Ruled out due to: Acute nature of the injury and complete lack of deep fluctuance, massive edema, or high fever.

Neuroma/stress fracture - Ruled out due to: Direct acute marine trauma history and visual confirmation of an entry wound and foreign material.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

Marine punctures carry an extraordinarily high risk of retaining brittle organic material and introducing highly virulent pathogens (e.g., Vibrio species). Bedside clinical assessment confidently localized the retained fragment superficially and excluded systemic envenomation. Urgent after-hours assessment was clinically required to execute immediate, sterile extraction of the marine spine and initiate targeted antibiotic prophylaxis, thereby avoiding a devastating deep space foot infection and preventing an unnecessary emergency department visit.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered local anesthesia. Performed delicate forceps extraction of the visible marine spine, ensuring complete removal without fragmentation. Wound irrigated copiously with sterile saline. Tetanus prophylaxis updated. Prescribed a course of Doxycycline/Ciprofloxacin (local marine flora coverage).
Positive Behavioral Change: Soak the foot in hot water (not scalding) for 20 minutes twice daily. The heat actively denatures residual marine proteins and toxins in the wound, directly reducing the intense burning pain and promoting local vasodilation for healing.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Routine GP review tomorrow for plain foot radiographs or high-resolution bedside ultrasound to definitively ensure no radiolucent/echogenic fragments remain.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Present to the Emergency Department immediately if the foot becomes massively swollen, intensely hot, if red streaks track up the ankle, or if you develop systemic fever, vomiting, or muscle cramps.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Sea urchin spines are highly brittle; microscopic fragments can occasionally remain and cause delayed granulomas despite careful extraction.
Clinical Course: The sharp burning will subside significantly within 24 hours. The puncture site will remain tender to direct pressure for 3 to 5 days.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek emergency medical attention if the pain dramatically worsens tomorrow, or if you lose feeling in any of your toes.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Marine bacteria are highly aggressive. The specific antibiotics prescribed target these unique water-borne pathogens before they can establish a deep tissue infection.
Actionable Steps: Take the full course of antibiotics. Get the requested x-ray tomorrow. Do not walk barefoot. Soak the foot in hot water to manage residual pain.

